skill: "sprint-backlog-grooming"
sections[3]:
  - "# sprint-backlog-grooming\n\nTurn a raw backlog into a sprint-ready one: definition-of-ready enforcement, oversized story splitting and an explicit dependency map. Use before sprint planning, when items sit in backlog for more than two sprints, or when planning stalls on unclear scope. Feeds kanban-agent-pipeline and conductor-status with clean, ordered work.\n\nOwner: role-scrum-agile-coach (pod-build, product). Direct human technical prose; zero conversational fluff.\n\n## Inputs and Outputs\n\nContract in TOON, as consumed by the runbook:\n\n```\nallowed_tools[4]:\n  - read_file\n  - write_file\n  - run_command\n  - ask_question\ninputs: \"backlog export backlog-export.toon from the board state server (Jira or Linear); definition-of-ready dor.toon; sprint calendar sprint-calendar.toon\"\noutputs: \"agents/artifacts/product/grooming-log.toon with split_items[], dependency_edges[], ready_items[] and blocked_items[]; handoff to the sprint planning session\"\n```\n\nThe agent refuses to run with missing or stale inputs: it stops and emits an ask_question naming the missing file instead of inventing values.\n"
  - "## Workflow\n\n1. Export the current backlog through the board state server; freeze the snapshot so grooming works on one consistent set.\n2. Run the definition-of-ready checklist per item: acceptance criteria present, sizing estimate present, no open blocking question, owner role named.\n3. Mark every failing item blocked_items[] with the exact failed clause; these are never planned into a sprint.\n4. Detect oversized items: any item estimated above 13 points or spanning more than one team boundary is a split candidate; propose 2-4 children with their own acceptance criteria.\n5. Build the dependency graph from item references; run cycle detection with run_command and report any cycle as a planning defect that must be cut, not worked around.\n6. Topologically sort ready_items[] so the plan order respects dependencies; annotate cross-team edges with the owning role.\n7. Emit ask_question to the product owner for every split that changes scope and every cycle that needs a cut decision.\n8. Write grooming-log.toon; append a two-line summary per item: what changed and why.\n9. Hand the ready list to kanban-agent-pipeline ordering and to conductor-status so dispatch consumes the same order.\n"
  - "## Handoff\n\nready list to kanban-agent-pipeline and conductor-status; scope decisions to product owner.\n"
